1. axe sweep (3 ranges x 2 viewports x 2 themes = 12 runs), each with all 3 supporting tabs
   clicked, every <details> forced open, and the trends toggle expanded when present:
   `node audit.js` → all 12 combinations report `violations=0`. Toggle present (`hadToggle=true`)
   on the 41-category (Jan–Aug) and June ranges; absent (≤12 rows, no cap) on the default range.
   Zero violations means nothing to attribute — RF3 introduces none. Full JSON in
   rf3-a11y/axe-results.json.

2. Point 1 (heading structure) / "lead is a <p> not a heading":
   `node headings.js` on the 41-category range at 1280px: exactly one `<h1>` ("Insights");
   outline is h1 → h2(What changed) → h3(Largest contributors) → h2(Review these
   transactions) → h2(Detected recurring spending) → h3×3(group headings) → h2(Supporting
   charts and tables) → h3×5(tab-panel headings, all bumped text-lg per RF3.B) — no skipped
   levels anywhere, including the new/bumped h2→h3 pairs. `leadTag: "P"`, `h1count: 1`.
   `node sibling.js`: `#period-context`'s `nextElementSibling` is `#insights-lead` (P) —
   satisfies RF3 acceptance-4 ("#insights-lead is the first element after #period-context")
   and constitution point 1 (logical order, no heading injected as decoration).

3. Points 3/9 (toggle + summaries — operable, stated role, visible focus, order):
   `node keyboard.js` (light+dark): toggle is `<button type="button" data-trends-toggle
   aria-expanded="false">`; focusing it and pressing Enter flips
   `aria-expanded="true"` and text to "Show the largest 12"; pressing Space flips it back
   to `aria-expanded="false"` / "Show all 41 categories" — both native activation keys work
   (plain `<button>`, no keydown hijacking). Focus ring on the button: light
   `outline:none` overridden by `outlineWidth:3px outlineColor:rgb(79,70,229)` (computed
   3px indigo ring — not `outline: none` with no replacement); dark
   `outlineColor:rgb(165,180,252)`, both clearly visible against their card
   backgrounds (see point 7 ratios below — this is a global focus-ring style, not new to
   RF3, but RF3's new button inherits it correctly). The two new `<summary>` elements
   ("About these findings", "About these estimates") are plain `<summary>`: native
   `outline:auto 1px` focus ring in both themes, and `Enter` opens the `<details>`
   (`summary open after Enter: true` in both themes) — no JS involved, so keyboard/AT
   behavior is the browser's native disclosure semantics.
   Focus order: by source order (confirmed against
   `insights-investigation.html`), the toggle `<button>` sits immediately after the closing
   `</div>` of the `.overflow-x-auto` scroll region and before the trends-table define's
   `{{end}}` — i.e. after the table region in both DOM and visual order, so Tab reaches it
   right after the table, matching the contract's placement requirement.

4. Point 16 / progressive enhancement: `curl` of the rendered 41-category page shows
   `data-trend-overflow="1"` on exactly 29 rows (41-12) and zero occurrences of the word
   `hidden` inside the trends-table markup (the 15 `hidden` hits in the full page are all
   pre-existing: desktop nav `hidden xl:flex`, light/dark icon-swap `hidden dark:block` /
   `block dark:hidden`, mobile-nav panel, `aria-hidden="true"` decorative SVGs, and the
   pre-existing `<input type="hidden" name="preset">` — none inside `#insights-panel-trends`).
   `node jsoff.js` with `javaScriptEnabled: false`: all 41 `<tbody>` rows report
   `hidden: false` / `display` not `none` (`hiddenRows: 0`), and the toggle button is present
   but `display: inline-block` (rendered, inert). This matches acceptance point 16 exactly:
   server markup carries no `hidden`; JS-off users see every row.
   OBSERVATION (not a FAIL): with JS off, the "Show all 41 categories" button does nothing
   when clicked — but since every row is already visible in that state, the button's label
   is not misleading (nothing is hidden to reveal) and clicking is a harmless no-op rather
   than a broken affordance. Point 3 ("buttons act") is arguably in tension with an inert
   button, but the constitution's actual requirement (point 16, parity between visual and
   AT state) is fully met since visual and AT state are identical and both already show
   everything. I read this as compliant, not a FAIL; flagging for the lead as a design note
   (a `<noscript>`-hidden button would avoid even the appearance of dead UI, but that is a
   preference, not a violation).

5. Point 7/12, NEW elements only, both themes, actual computed backgrounds via
   `getComputedStyle` walk-up (real built CSS, not guessed): `node contrast.js` on the
   41-category range, light then dark, trends tab active, all `<details>` open —
   `#insights-lead` (text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100) on its ancestor `bg-gray-50`-ish body
   background: light `rgb(28,25,23)` on `rgb(245,245,244)` = 16.03:1; dark `rgb(245,245,244)`
   on `rgb(28,25,23)` = 16.03:1. The trends toggle and both new `<summary>`s (`text-accent`)
   on their card/body backgrounds: light 5.76–6.29:1, dark 7.61–8.77:1. All comfortably clear
   4.5:1 in both themes — no theme-specific invisibility.

6. Point 11 (chart text alternative) with 29/41 rows capped by default: the constitution's
   own text explicitly permits the data table alternative to be "collapsed behind a
   disclosure." The trends-table cap + `<button aria-expanded>` behaves exactly like a
   disclosure — sighted and AT users get identical information at every state (12 rows
   collapsed, all rows expanded via the same control, same button text conveys state to
   both). I read point 11 as satisfied, not violated: this is an OBSERVATION, not a FAIL.

7. Point 10, HTMX swap with toggle expanded: `node htmxswap.js` — expand the toggle
   (`aria-expanded:true`, `hiddenCount:0`), click a date preset (`1m`), wait for the HTMX
   swap. After: toggle re-renders `aria-expanded:false` (cap re-applied, `hiddenCount:12`)
   and `document.activeElement` is the clicked preset `<button data-preset="1m">` itself
   (`inSwapped:true` but it is NOT replaced by the swap — the date-filter form is explicitly
   out of RF3's territory). Focus is not lost to `<body>`, satisfying point 10's "focus
   restored to a sensible element inside the swapped region."
   OBSERVATION: the trends-toggle's expanded state resets to collapsed on every preset swap
   (new server render always starts `aria-expanded="false"`). This is consistent and
   predictable (matches the `applyTrendsCap()`-on-`htmx:afterSwap` contract in RF.3.D) and
   is not a regression — nothing claims persistence across swaps. Not a FAIL.

8. 390px, both themes, toggle expanded: `node overflow.js` — `document.documentElement`
   `scrollWidth === clientWidth` (390 === 390, `overflow:false`) in both light and dark; the
   trends table itself is 640px wide (`min-w-[640px]`, pixel-pinned per RF1 note) and scrolls
   inside its own `.overflow-x-auto` region (`regionClientWidth:322`) rather than the page —
   no new overflow introduced by RF3's cap/toggle.

